I am trying to write data to SQL tables using transactions so that if any of the insertion fails, I want to revert all insertions done as part of transactions. Following is the code 

tryCatch(
dbWithTransaction(conn, {
  dbExecute(conn, "INSERT INTO trail (speed, dist) VALUES (1, 1);")
  dbExecute(conn, "INSERT INTO trail (speed, dist) VALUES (2 2);")
  dbExecute(conn, "INSERT INTO trail (speed, dist) VALUES (3, 3);")
}),
error = function(){
  dbRollback(conn)
}
)
dbDisconnect(conn)

Since I have introduced error intentionally in second statement, I expect transaction rollback.
In actual, RStudio is locking trail table which can be seen through sp_who2. until and unless, RStudio session is quit, we'll not be able to perform any operations around trail table.

How to perform rollback on transaction failure?

Could these be added? Something like this should do it:

```
setMethod("dbBuildIndex", signature("DBIConnection", "character"), 
          function(conn, tablename, cols, indexname = NULL, ...) {
            if (is.null(indexname)) {
              indexname <- paste(paste0(cols, collapse = ""), "idx", sep = "_")
            }
            statement = paste("CREATE INDEX", indexname, "ON", tablename, 
                              "(", paste0(cols, collapse=","), ")", sep = " ")
            rs <- dbGetQuery(conn, statement, ...)
          }
)
```

I can send a pull request if you are interested.

Something like this:

``` R
withTransaction <- function(con, fun, ...) {
  dbBegin(con)
  tryCatch(
    {
      out <- fun(con)
      dbCommit(con)
    },
    error = function(e){
      dbRollback(e)
      stop(e)
    }
  )

  out
}
```

Ensures that rollback happens automatically on error - otherwise this is quite a lot of boilerplate to get correct

(I don't think this needs to be a generic, but I'm not 100% sure)

I feel that the prose for the DBI specification is best suited in this package, because it is actually useful for DBI users. The prose is tightly connected to the specification code, but isolated from the actual testing logic, which can stay in DBItest for now.

One minor obstacle is that we don't want to introduce import dependencies, but the spec code requires `testthat`. We could make retrieving the actual spec code conditional on the presence of the `testthat` package.

@hadley: Do you have a strong opinion here?

A stop() in the transacted code is rethrown after rollback, we need a special condition that we could catch. This could be the same as for #111:

```
dbAbort <- function() stop(structure(list(message = "Abort"), class = c("dbi_abort", "condition"))

...
tryCatch({
  res <- code
  dbCommit(...)
  res
},
dbi_abort = function(e) { dbRollback(); message(conditionMessage(e)) },
error = function(e) { dbRollback(); stop(e) }
)

...

dbWithTransaction(con, {
  do_stuff()
  if (some_test()) dbAbort()
  do_more_stuff()
})
```

